/* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   RANSOMWARE ECOSYSTEM MAP : DATA FILE
   ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Monthly data updates happen ONLY in this file. The map page
   (the current map page) is never edited for
   data updates; it reads this file automatically via
   <script src="data/ecosystem-map-data.js"></script>.

   What lives here:
     NODES          : the 35 ecosystem nodes, including each node's
                      monthlyEvents array (this is where monthly
                      EDP report deltas get appended)
     EDGES          : the dependency connections between nodes
     ANNEX_DATA     : Intel Annex records (collection posture,
                      methods, indicators) keyed by node id
     MODULE_DOCS    : EDP module PDF references keyed by module id
     MODULE_ALIASES : maps alias module ids (M11b, M13b, M13c) to
                      their canonical MODULE_DOCS entry

   How to add a monthly event: find the node in NODES and append to
   its monthlyEvents array an object of the form
     {month:'2026-07',status:'DISRUPTED',label:'...',detail:'...',conf:'CONFIRMED'}
   Valid status values: DISRUPTED, DEGRADED, RECONSTITUTING, NOTABLE.
   The header delta window and the delta feed panel recompute
   themselves from these arrays; no page edits needed.
   ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */

const NODES = [
  { id:'bph',      conf:'CONFIRMED',      label:['Bulletproof','Hosting'],     module:'M09', nodeRef:'Node 03', layer:0, tier:'CRITICAL', replace:'HIGH',   backfire:'LOW-MED', protected:true,  owner:'FVEY / providers',   phase:'A', jurisdiction:'western',    desc:'Abuse-resistant hosting backbone. Provides persistent infrastructure to forums, leak sites, loader panels, and service continuity.', why:'The most cross-cutting infrastructure node in the ecosystem. Every node requiring persistent online presence depends on BPH directly or indirectly. BPH disruption degrades multiple nodes simultaneously rather than one at a time. The November 2025 OFAC designation of Media Land and Zservers established a replicable multi-jurisdictional model, but financial isolation without concurrent upstream ISP depeering allows continued operation. By July 2026 the Media Land model escalated: the EU designated Media Land LLC and ML.Cloud with owner Volosovik (Jul 13), and the next day DOJ (N.D. Ohio) unsealed a December 2024 indictment of Volosovik, Zatolokin, and Pankova alongside both companies (44 victims enumerated, over $62M in losses) with a concurrent Rewards for Justice offer of up to $10M. The replicable model is now sanctions plus indictment plus reward, not sanctions alone (Confirmed). SORM update (May 2026): Mintsifry Order No. 1174 extended SORM obligations to all operators of technological networks holding ASNs or IP block identifiers — the class that includes Russian commercial hosting providers like Selectel. FSB now has formalized intercept access to infrastructure hosted on these networks without a court order. The framing that Russian-hosted BPH represents lower Western LE cooperation responsiveness remains accurate; it must now be paired with the analytical opposite: FSB has comprehensive technical visibility into the same infrastructure it declines to disrupt. Non-enforcement by FSB against Russian-hosted BPH supporting foreign-targeting ransomware is a documented deliberate choice, not a surveillance gap.', hit:'Upstream provider pressure, registrar and ASN and CDN targeting, infrastructure takedown, upstream ISP depeering on the McColo model.', damage:'Stable criminal infrastructure collapses into noisier fallback hosting. All dependent nodes face simultaneous migration cost and operational disruption.', successor:'Gray-market VPS resellers, alternate abuse-tolerant providers, Aeza Group and BEARHOST and Voodoo Servers as documented successor candidates.', monthlyEvents:[{month:'2026-03',status:'DEGRADED',label:'Media Land / Aeza / Hypercore sanctions chain active',detail:'OFAC/UK/Australia sanctioned Media Land LLC (Nov 2025) for supporting LockBit, BlackSuit, and Play. Aeza Group rebranded as UK-front Hypercore to evade sanctions. BPH operations continuing under successor entities. Financial isolation without concurrent ISP depeering allows continued operation.',conf:'CONFIRMED'},{month:'2026-05',status:'DISRUPTED',label:'Stark Industries FIOD seizure: 800+ servers, 2 arrests under Dutch sanctions law',detail:'Stark Industries / WorkTitans BV (Dutch FIOD, May 18-27): 800+ servers seized at 5 Dutch data center locations; 2 arrests, Youssef Zinad (57, Amsterdam) and Andrey Nesterenko (39, The Hague), charged under Dutch sanctions law (knowingly providing resources to EU-sanctioned entities), a novel prosecutorial theory that if sustained enables prosecution of EU hosting providers without requiring proof of cybercrime commission. Historical BPH migration window: 1-7 days. The simultaneous Operation Saffron action against First VPN is tracked on the VPN / Anonymity Providers node; cross-reference of Stark server logs against the First VPN 506-user list is the highest-priority near-term action (window closes ~June 10, 2026).',conf:'CONFIRMED'},{month:'2026-06',status:'RECONSTITUTING',label:'Stark successor signal: THE.Hosting under WorkTitans B.V., ASN migration to AS209847 (UNCONFIRMED)',detail:'Reporting indicates sanctioned Stark Industries / PQ Hosting rebranded in June to THE.Hosting under WorkTitans B.V., migrating AS44477 to AS209847 roughly one month after the May 18-27 Dutch seizure of 800+ servers. Primary source gated; the ASN and WorkTitans linkage is UNCONFIRMED pending independent WHOIS/ASN attribution. If confirmed: sanction WorkTitans B.V. and de-peer AS209847 within the 30-60 day window before stable peering and customer migration complete.',conf:'CREDIBLE'},{month:'2026-07',status:'DEGRADED',label:'Media Land escalation: EU designation, N.D. Ohio indictment unsealed, $10M RFJ reward',detail:'The EU designated Media Land LLC and ML.Cloud with owner Volosovik in the July 13 joint EU/UK cyber package (9 individuals and 4 entities under the EU cyber regime). On July 14 DOJ (N.D. Ohio) unsealed a December 2024 indictment (Case 1:24-CR-001161) of Volosovik, Zatolokin, and Pankova alongside Medialand LLC and ML.Cloud LLC: conspiracy to commit computer fraud, wire fraud, and money laundering, 44 victims enumerated, over $62M in losses, with a concurrent Rewards for Justice offer of up to $10M for information on foreign-government-linked use of the companies. All three individuals at large in Russia; no extradition treaty. The BPH pressure model is now sanctions plus indictment plus reward.',conf:'CONFIRMED'}] },
  { id:'vpn',      conf:'CONFIRMED',      label:['VPN / Anonymity','Providers'],   module:'M09', nodeRef:'Node 03b', layer:0, tier:'MEDIUM', replace:'MEDIUM', backfire:'LOW', protected:false, owner:'FVEY / LE / OFAC', phase:null, jurisdiction:'western', desc:'Anonymization layer: no-logs VPN and proxy services sold to criminal customers, masking operator and affiliate infrastructure across the intrusion chain.', why:'A distinct enabler with its own customer model: subscription anonymity sold to individual operators and affiliates, rather than hosting sold to services. First VPN Service (1VPNS) provided anonymization to the ecosystem for 12+ years with a no-logs policy and refusal of law enforcement cooperation, servicing clients across 25+ ransomware groups. The May 2026 Operation Saffron takedown (33 servers across 27 countries, 506 users identified across 25 groups, 83 intelligence packages shared) was the most significant anonymization infrastructure removal since Operation Cronos, and the July 13, 2026 OFAC designation of 1VPNS and its administrator Rashevskyi, coordinated with UK FCDO, is the first designation of a VPN provider as a ransomware enabler (Confirmed). The customer-list exploitation path parallels the Smokeloader demand-side model: subscribers now carry personal exposure.', hit:'Server seizure with customer identification (Saffron model), OFAC designation of provider and administrators with wallet mapping across eight blockchains, demand-side follow-up against identified subscribers.', damage:'Operators lose a trusted anonymity layer; connections fall back to commercial VPNs with logging exposure, Tor latency, or compromised proxies, raising attribution risk across every dependent node.', successor:'Commercial no-KYC VPNs, residential proxy networks, successor criminal-facing providers rebuilding customer trust from zero.', monthlyEvents:[{month:'2026-05',status:'DISRUPTED',label:'Operation Saffron dismantles First VPN: 33 servers, 27 countries, 506 users identified',detail:'Operation Saffron (May 19-20, France/Netherlands/Europol, 18 countries): First VPN dismantled; 33 servers seized across 27 countries, 1 arrested (Ukraine), 506 users identified across 25 ransomware groups (Avaddon, Phobos, and others), 83 intelligence packages shared with partner agencies. First VPN had provided anonymization for the ecosystem for 12+ years; the most significant anonymization infrastructure removal since Operation Cronos. FBI Boston supported the European action.',conf:'CONFIRMED'},{month:'2026-06',status:'NOTABLE',label:'FBI Operation Riptide (Jun 9-10): 60-day whole-of-FBI campaign, First VPN named target',detail:'All 56 FBI field offices implementing Executive Order 14390: search warrants, indictments, worldwide arrests, and crypto seizures to date; First VPN (alleged to have serviced 25+ ransomware groups) named as a target. Dollar and arrest totals undisclosed; assessed as a strategic umbrella over discrete actions, ecosystem impact Medium. Single-source reporting (BeInCrypto).',conf:'CREDIBLE'},{month:'2026-07',status:'DEGRADED',label:'OFAC designates First VPN Service (1VPNS) and administrator Rashevskyi (Jul 13)',detail:'OFAC designated VPN provider 1VPNS (ransomware clients since 2014, no-logs policy, refuses law enforcement cooperation) and its administrator Dmytro Rashevskyi (aliases Maksim Sorin, Roman Chabanenko) under E.O. 13694 as amended and in furtherance of E.O. 14390, coordinated with UK FCDO, following the May takedown of 1VPNS infrastructure. Chainalysis reports wallet addresses identified for 1VPNS and Rashevskyi across Bitcoin, Ethereum, Litecoin, Zcash, Dash, TRON, Dogecoin, and Solana. First designation of a VPN provider as a ransomware enabler; individuals at large.',conf:'CONFIRMED'}] },
  { id:'forums',   conf:'CONFIRMED',   label:['Underground','Forums'],      module:'M10', nodeRef:'Node 07', layer:0, tier:'HIGH',     replace:'HIGH',   backfire:'LOW',     protected:false, owner:'LE / private intel',  phase:'B', jurisdiction:'constrained', desc:'Market governance and trust infrastructure, including recruitment, vetting, dispute resolution, and reputation.', why:'Criminal institutional backbone for the entire ecosystem. The 19-day median lag from IAB forum listing to DLS victim publication makes forums a measurable precursor indicator of imminent attacks and a high-value early-warning collection node. Russian-language institutional forums function as multi-decade criminal institutions effectively impervious to external disruption without Russian government cooperation.', hit:'Forum seizure, infiltration, trust-node disruption, reputation attacks, escrow service designation as a low-backfire Phase B compounding action.', damage:'Trust declines, recruiting slows, and criminal market friction rises.', successor:'Telegram splinters, replacement forums, invite-only spaces.', monthlyEvents:[{month:'2026-03',status:'DISRUPTED',label:'RAMP forum seized — FBI (Jan 28, 2026)',detail:'FBI seized RAMP on Jan 28 — the only dark web forum where RaaS affiliate recruitment was explicitly permitted. Hosted LockBit, ALPHV, Conti, DragonForce, Qilin, RansomHub. Admin confirmed seizure; 340K IP records leaked. Migration to closed T1erOne ($450 entry/referral) and Rehub ongoing.',conf:'CONFIRMED'},{month:'2026-03',status:'DISRUPTED',label:'Operation Leak: LeakBase dismantled — FBI/Europol (Mar 3-4)',detail:'FBI and Europol dismantled LeakBase in a 14-country operation: 142K members, 215K+ messages, hundreds of millions of credentials. 13 arrests, 32 searches. Full database in FBI custody. 60-90 day recovery expected as XSS, Exploit.in, and Telegram channels absorb demand.',conf:'CONFIRMED'},{month:'2026-04',status:'RECONSTITUTING',label:'T1erOne emerging as RAMP successor; BreachForums embedded in Vect RaaS',detail:'T1erOne ($450 entry or verified referral) and Rehub absorbing displaced RAMP affiliates. BreachForums embedded as operational infrastructure for Vect RaaS — 300K+ members received affiliate keys Apr 16. Recruitment migrating to closed, referral-gated channels, reducing open-source visibility.',conf:'CONFIRMED'},{month:'2026-05',status:'DISRUPTED',label:'RAMP dark 120+ days — anomalous non-reconstitution; BreachForums DB exposure',detail:'RAMP remains dark at 120+ days post-seizure (as of May 31, 2026) — the longest absence since the forum launched in 2021. Typical reconstitution window is 60-90 days. Competing explanations: (1) LE infiltration deterring reconstitution; (2) permanent migration to private invite-only channels, which is operationally more concerning as it reduces open-source visibility into ecosystem coordination. 180-day reconstitution status due July 28, 2026. BreachForums: 323,986-member database resurfaced publicly; platform operational under degraded trust. DarkForums grew 600% during the BreachForums/RAMP disruption period, absorbing premium IAB listings.',conf:'CONFIRMED'},{month:'2026-06',status:'NOTABLE',label:'RAMP anomalously dark past 150 days; LeakBase not reconstituted at ~90 days: durable forum-layer cost',detail:'RAMP remains dark roughly 150 days after the Jan 28 FBI seizure, versus the typical 60-90 day takedown-to-relaunch gap; LeakBase is not reconstituted at roughly 90 days. Lengthening reconstitution cycles indicate enforcement is imposing durable cost on the forum layer even as RaaS victim volume climbs; the sharpest contrast is stealer infrastructure, where Lumma fully reconstituted within weeks of its 2025 takedown. Competing explanation: voluntary operator dormancy to evade heat. Medium confidence.',conf:'ANALYST'}] },
  { id:'crypters', conf:'CREDIBLE', label:['Crypters &','Packers'],      module:'M03', nodeRef:'Node 11', layer:1, tier:'MEDIUM',  replace:'LOW',    backfire:'LOW',     protected:false, owner:'Private sector',      phase:null, jurisdiction:'western',    desc:'Payload obfuscation layer used to evade AV and EDR before distribution.', why:'Force multiplier across multiple stages simultaneously without requiring Russian infrastructure access or FSB protection triggers. No major LE operation has specifically targeted CaaS infrastructure at scale despite the Endgame campaign model establishing the template. The FUD Kill Chain is a confirmed mechanism not yet operationalized at scale. The July 13, 2026 OFAC designation of Belarusian cryptor vendor Silayev is the first enforcement action of any kind against a crypter vendor (Confirmed); it is a designation, not an infrastructure operation, so the at-scale gap remains.', hit:'Signature development and provider disruption, honeypot stub acquisition for AV/EDR partner sharing, operator identification and OFAC designation using ransomware customer nexus.', damage:'Payloads burn faster, campaigns need more maintenance, and the cost of maintaining FUD status rises across stealers and loaders simultaneously.', successor:'Commodity crypter vendors. Market concentration means operator-level disruption is more durable than family-level takedowns.', monthlyEvents:[{month:'2026-07',status:'NOTABLE',label:'First crypter-vendor designation: OFAC designates Silayev (Jul 13)',detail:'OFAC designated Belarusian cryptor provider Yevgeniy Silayev under E.O. 13694 as amended and in furtherance of E.O. 14390, coordinated with UK FCDO, in the same action that designated First VPN Service. First enforcement action against a crypter vendor, part of the July 2026 shift of personnel exposure onto the enabler layer (crypter vendor, VPN provider, MaaS operators, BPH operators all named in one wave). Designation-only, no custody component; individual at large.',conf:'CONFIRMED'}] },
  { id:'callers',  conf:'CREDIBLE',  label:['Callers &','Spammers'],      module:'M04', nodeRef:'No node', layer:1, tier:'MEDIUM',  replace:'MEDIUM', backfire:'LOW',     protected:false, owner:'Platform pressure',   phase:null, jurisdiction:'western',    desc:'Human-layer access generation through spam, fake support, and social engineering.', why:'Human-layer bypass that substitutes for technical access when technical methods are blocked. Uniquely among all ecosystem nodes, the primary disruption lever is defensive hardening on the victim side (cryptographic or multi-party helpdesk verification), not LE action against infrastructure.', hit:'Platform bans, telecom abuse pressure, takedowns of VaaS platforms, arrest of call-center operators within Western LE jurisdiction.', damage:'Lower-skill access generation throughput drops. Social engineering-dependent operations become structurally harder when verification controls are mandatory.', successor:'New spam crews and call-center operators.', monthlyEvents:[] },
  { id:'loaders',  conf:'CONFIRMED',  label:['Loaders &','Botnets'],       module:'M02', nodeRef:'Node 05', layer:2, tier:'HIGH',    replace:'HIGH',   backfire:'LOW-MED', protected:false, owner:'FVEY / LE',          phase:'C', jurisdiction:'western',    desc:'Delivery and execution layer. Drops or executes second-stage payloads on compromised hosts at scale.', why:'Distribution infrastructure is the highest-durability target within this node. SEO poisoning networks and IoT C2 layers take months to rebuild. The Endgame campaign model is the correct template but has a critical gap: distribution infrastructure was not a co-equal target alongside C2 seizure.', hit:'Sinkholing, C2 disruption, coordinated botnet operations, upstream ISP notification for IoT and NAS C2 layers, coordinated de-indexing of SEO delivery networks.', damage:'Mass delivery degrades and campaigns become more manual.', successor:'Successor botnets and alternate loaders. Loader code is LOW replace difficulty. Distribution infrastructure is HIGH replace difficulty.', monthlyEvents:[{month:'2026-03',status:'NOTABLE',label:'Ilya Angelov (Russian botnet operator) sentenced 24 months',detail:'Russian national Ilya Angelov, 40, sentenced to 24 months for operating TA551-linked botnet used by ransomware gangs. FBI identified 70+ US corporations infected via the botnet; $14M+ in extortion payments attributed.',conf:'CONFIRMED'},{month:'2026-04',status:'NOTABLE',label:'TeamPCP supply chain attacks; formal Vect RaaS partnership',detail:'TeamPCP attributed to supply chain attacks on Checkmarx, SAP npm, Aqua Security Trivy, LiteLLM AI proxy, and OpenVSX. Formal Vect RaaS partnership announced — TeamPCP provides supply-chain-sourced initial access feeding Vect affiliates. 1,000+ downstream enterprise environments estimated affected.',conf:'CONFIRMED'},{month:'2026-05',status:'NOTABLE',label:'Mini Shai-Hulud CI/CD worm (TeamPCP/CipherForce); Glassworm; DAEMON Tools trojanized installer',detail:'Three confirmed supply chain incidents in May 2026 — Verizon 2026 DBIR notes 30% of all breaches now involve a third-party supplier (double the 2024 rate). Mini Shai-Hulud (May 10-11): TeamPCP (linked to CipherForce ransomware group) compromised TanStack/router GitHub repo; worm propagated to 42 @tanstack/* packages, spreading to 317 total packages across Alibaba Antv and others in ~20 minutes; 630 malicious versions published; Mistral AI, UiPath, 170+ downstream packages affected; 2 OpenAI employee devices compromised; credentials exfiltrated from GitHub Actions, AWS IMDS, HashiCorp Vault, Kubernetes. Attack exploited pull_request_target OIDC misconfiguration to produce malicious packages with valid SLSA provenance — bypassing post-SolarWinds supply chain integrity controls. Glassworm (disrupted May 27 by CrowdStrike/Google): 300+ GitHub repositories poisoned, developer credential theft at scale. DAEMON Tools installer compromise (Apr 8 - late Apr 2026): legitimately signed trojanized installers distributed from official vendor website for ~2.5 weeks, bypassing all reputation-based defenses. TeamPCP/CipherForce link means CI/CD attack vector may directly feed ransomware deployments.',conf:'CONFIRMED'},{month:'2026-06',status:'DISRUPTED',label:'Endgame June phase hits the loader front end: Amadey and SocGholish actioned',detail:'The June 15-24 Operation Endgame phase targeted the loader and stealer front end of the ransomware supply chain rather than end-stage payloads. Amadey (v5.87; 1,837 samples YTD 2026; its largest botnet cluster delivered Lumma, Vidar, StealC, RedLine, SmokeLoader, XWorm, AsyncRAT, and Rhadamanthys across 53 clusters) and SocGholish fake-browser-update infrastructure were dismantled alongside StealC.',conf:'CONFIRMED'}] },
  { id:'stealers', conf:'CREDIBLE', label:['Stealers &','Log Markets'],  module:'M01', nodeRef:'Node 10', layer:2, tier:'MEDIUM',  replace:'LOW-MED',backfire:'LOW',     protected:false, owner:'LE / vendors',        phase:null, jurisdiction:'western',    desc:'Credential theft and session-log markets feeding access brokers.', why:'Commodity input for the credential-based access economy. Three top vendors supply the majority of Russian Market inventory at MEDIUM replace difficulty — vendor-level disruption is more durable than family-level takedowns. Disruption value compounds when timed with IAB market pressure within a 60-90 day sequenced window.', hit:'Market disruption, notification and credential invalidation, vendor-level identification and designation, enterprise-scale identity hardening to devalue log inventory.', damage:'Access quality falls, some low-end intrusion paths dry up, and IAB market prices rise as supply quality declines.', successor:'Fresh stealer families and replacement log markets.', monthlyEvents:[{month:'2026-03',status:'NOTABLE',label:'Lumma fully reconstituted after May 2025 takedown',detail:'Lumma infostealer disrupted by Microsoft-led action (May 2025, 2,300 domains seized) — fully reconstituted by March 2026. Operating at scale via ClickFix and CastleLoader delivery chains. C2 infrastructure on Russian cloud providers (Selectel). New MFA bypass capabilities added.',conf:'CONFIRMED'},{month:'2026-04',status:'NOTABLE',label:'Remus stealer active as Lumma successor; EtherHiding C2',detail:'Remus stealer active since Feb 2026 as Lumma successor. Uses EtherHiding (Ethereum blockchain-based C2 resolution) to resist domain sinkholing — hardens against traditional takedown methodology. Developer partially doxxed Aug-Oct 2025. Estimated 40-60% of corporate credential pipeline. [ANALYST INFERENCE, medium confidence]',conf:'CREDIBLE REPORTING'},{month:'2026-05',status:'NOTABLE',label:'Lumma C2 permanently on Selectel (Russian-hosted); 18.7M logs Jan-May 2026',detail:'Lumma Stealer C2 infrastructure migrated from Cloudflare to Selectel (commercially registered Russian hosting provider) following May 2025 disruption. This moves C2 to a jurisdiction with documented lower Western LE cooperation responsiveness, materially reducing probability of a successful repeat takedown. Note (May 2026): Selectel-class Russian hosting providers now fall within the scope of Mintsifry Order No. 1174 SORM obligations — FSB has formalized intercept access to this infrastructure class. Lower Western LE responsiveness and comprehensive FSB technical visibility are the simultaneous analytical reality for Russian-hosted C2. FSB non-disruption of Lumma C2 on Selectel is deliberate non-enforcement, not a visibility gap. Flare 2026 State of Enterprise Infostealer Identity Exposure (May 25): 18.7 million infostealer logs collected Jan-May 2026 in one sample study; 54% of ransomware victims had domain credentials appear in stealer log marketplaces before the attack was detected at the victim organization. Chainalysis 2026 identifies IAB on-chain inflow spikes typically preceding ransomware payment spikes by ~30 days — a documented detection window for organizations monitoring their credential exposure.',conf:'CONFIRMED'},{month:'2026-06',status:'DISRUPTED',label:'Operation Endgame June phase: StealC, Amadey, SocGholish infrastructure dismantled (Jun 15-24)',detail:'Europol, BKA, and the Netherlands with Microsoft DCU actioned 326 servers and 142 domains, recovered 27 million stolen credentials, froze roughly EUR 41M in crypto, disrupted 200+ C2 servers, and identified 18,000+ victim machines. StealC v2.2.1 ($300/mo), Amadey v5.87, and Evil Corp-linked SocGholish (14,971 infected WordPress sites remediated) all actioned. Reconstitution watch: the Lumma precedent suggests C2 re-emergence within 30-60 days; developers remain at large. Pre-staging a second strike on successor C2 converts a one-time seizure into sustained denial.',conf:'CONFIRMED'},{month:'2026-07',status:'NOTABLE',label:'LummaC2 developers designated by EU and UK (Jul 13): MaaS operators personally named',detail:'The joint EU/UK cyber package designated LummaC2 developers Voronin and Gordienko (EU and UK) plus UK-only third designee Zhurkin, the first simultaneous EU and UK action under their respective cyber regimes. NCA reports at least 2,100 UK Lumma Stealer victims in the prior six months. Personnel exposure applied to stealer developers who remained at large through the 2025 infrastructure takedown that Lumma fully reconstituted from within weeks; asset freezes plus travel bans, all at large.',conf:'CONFIRMED'}] },
  { id:'exploits', conf:'CREDIBLE', label:['Exploit','Brokers'],         module:'M06', nodeRef:'Node 16', layer:2, tier:'CRITICAL',    replace:'HIGH', backfire:'LOW-MED', protected:false, owner:'Policy lead / CISA-NSA / FVEY IC',             phase:'P', jurisdiction:'constrained', desc:'Exploit and zero-day supply layer enabling credential-free intrusion paths.', why:'CRITICAL-tier policy-track node. A single 0-day acquisition can produce more victim access than months of IAB market activity. Primary disruption levers are coordinated vulnerability disclosure, patch velocity, bug bounty scaling, and only then screened attribution or designation where state adjacency risk is cleared. Dark Covenant 3.0 screening remains a hard prerequisite for any public attribution action due to state adjacency risk.', hit:'Coordinated vulnerability disclosure, exploit burn via enterprise patch velocity, KEV enforcement acceleration, scaled bug bounty reform, screened OFAC designation or attribution for confirmed criminal brokers.', damage:'High-value stealth access paths become scarcer, more expensive, and less reliable against hardened targets.', successor:'Parallel suppliers, direct researcher relationships, and state-adjacent channels.', monthlyEvents:[{month:'2026-03',status:'NOTABLE',label:'Interlock zero-day: CVE-2026-20131 Cisco FMC (CVSS 10.0)',detail:'Interlock ransomware exploited CVE-2026-20131 (Cisco Firepower Management Center) as zero-day for 36 days before public disclosure. Unauthenticated root RCE via crafted HTTP requests. Victims: DaVita (dialysis), Kettering Health, Texas Tech University, city of Saint Paul MN. CISA KEV: March 19, 2026.',conf:'CONFIRMED'},{month:'2026-05',status:'NOTABLE',label:'CVE-2026-20182 Cisco SD-WAN (CVSS 10.0) — 70-day exploitation gap; Oracle EBS zero-day on RAMP',detail:'Two CVSS 10.0 vulnerabilities with significant KEV gap issues. CVE-2026-20182 (Cisco Catalyst SD-WAN Controller): auth bypass enabling unauthenticated remote admin access; post-compromise: SSH key injection, NETCONF modification, root escalation, web shell deployment (XenShell, Godzilla, Behinder, Sliver C2, XMRig). Exploited by 10 distinct clusters including UAT-8616 (primary) since March 2026; added to CISA KEV May 14-15 — a ~70-day exploitation gap. CVE-2025-61882 (Oracle E-Business Suite v12.2.3-12.2.14): unauthenticated RCE via HTTP; zero-day sold on RAMP forum by a Cl0p-attributed actor as of late May 2026; NOT in CISA KEV as of May 31. Cl0p historical pattern (MOVEit, GoAnywhere, Citrix Bleed) involves zero-day purchase followed by simultaneous mass exploitation. Organizations relying solely on KEV for patch prioritization are exposed in these pre-KEV windows.',conf:'CONFIRMED'},{month:'2026-06',status:'NOTABLE',label:'June KEV adds: Check Point VPN zero-day (Qilin affiliate), BlueHammer ransomware-flagged, Oracle PeopleSoft zero-day',detail:'CVE-2026-50751 (Check Point VPN IKEv1 auth bypass, CVSS 9.3): several dozen organizations hit in an early-June spike, Qilin affiliate assessed at medium-high confidence, KEV Jun 8-9. CVE-2026-33825 (BlueHammer, Defender LPE): KEV-listed Apr 22 but only flagged as ransomware-exploited Jun 30, a 69-day listing-to-ransomware lag. CVE-2026-35273 (Oracle PeopleSoft zero-day): ShinyHunters data theft across 300+ instances and 100+ organizations, education-heavy; Nissan disclosed an employee-data breach Jun 29.',conf:'CONFIRMED'}] },
  { id:'iab',      conf:'CONFIRMED',      label:['Initial Access','Brokers'],  module:'M05', nodeRef:'Node 04', layer:3, tier:'HIGH',    replace:'MEDIUM', backfire:'LOW',     protected:false, owner:'FVEY / LE',          phase:'B', jurisdiction:'western',    desc:'Validated foothold brokers reselling access to ransomware affiliates at major markup.', why:'Most commercially mature segment of the supply chain. Rising prices and migration to vetted forums signal ecosystem resilience. Boutique IAB financial designation (USDT and TRON flow tracing) is the highest-durability targeted action — boutique operators maintain trusted RaaS buyer relationships that are difficult to reconstitute post-designation.', hit:'Undercover operations, market pressure, boutique operator financial designation via blockchain tracing, forum section takedown coordinated with Node 07 action.', damage:'Affiliate access pipeline shrinks and prices rise.', successor:'Broker fragmentation, self-sourced access, exploit fallback.', monthlyEvents:[{month:'2026-03',status:'NOTABLE',label:'Aleksei Volkov (Russian IAB) sentenced 81 months — SDIND',detail:'Russian IAB Aleksei Volkov, 26, sentenced to 81 months by Southern District of Indiana. Provided corporate network access to Yanluowang ransomware group. $9M actual losses, $24M intended losses, dozens of ransomware attacks on US organizations attributed.',conf:'CONFIRMED'},{month:'2026-04',status:'NOTABLE',label:'IAB market fragmented post-RAMP; median price $1,295',detail:'RAMP seizure (Jan 28) eliminated the primary IAB marketplace. Market migrating to T1erOne and private channels. Median access price: $1,295 (down ~60% from 2023). 58% of listings under $1,000. BravoX requiring proof of access to targets with >$5M revenue or financial deposit.',conf:'CONFIRMED'},{month:'2026-05',status:'NOTABLE',label:'Flare 2026: 18.7M logs Jan-May; 54% of ransomware victims had credentials in stealer markets pre-attack',detail:'Flare 2026 State of Enterprise Infostealer Identity Exposure (May 25): 18.7 million infostealer logs collected January-May 2026 in one sample study. 54% of ransomware victims had domain credentials appear in stealer log marketplaces before the attack was detected at the victim organization. Chainalysis 2026 documents IAB on-chain inflow spikes typically preceding ransomware payment spikes by approximately 30 days — a documented lead-time detection window. Fresh logs (under 48 hours old) command 3-5x commodity price; average commodity log ~$10/bot; corporate domain-admin credentials $100-$3,000+. The Gentlemen confirmed operating a dedicated credential channel (ЛБ logs / OWA/M365 stealer logs) — direct stealer-to-RaaS pipeline with no IAB intermediary, compressing attack chain. Rapid7 H2 2025: RDP 21.2% of IAB listings, VPN 12.8% (but 68% of actual remote-access exploit cases per S-RM 2026), RDWeb 11.2%.',conf:'CONFIRMED'}] },
  { id:'raas',     conf:'CONFIRMED',     label:['RaaS','Operator'],           module:'M07', nodeRef:'Node: Op',layer:4, tier:'HIGH',    replace:'MEDIUM', backfire:'HIGH',    protected:true,  owner:'Protected core',     phase:null, jurisdiction:'constrained', desc:'Franchise core that develops tooling, manages admin panels, and oversees affiliate recruitment.', why:'Central aggregation function that monetizes every upstream supply chain input. The 2024 ecosystem delivered 7,460+ named victims and $813M in confirmed payments despite two major LE operations. Non-payment rate growth (Coveware: greater than 70% for professionally supported victims) is the most structurally durable disruption mechanism available.', hit:'Only after protection weakening: panel seizure, trust destruction, financial payment rail pressure, covert access model before public seizure.', damage:'Brand confidence and coordination break if support layers are hit simultaneously.', successor:'Rebrand, splinter crews, successor franchise. Reconstitution is faster with each successive disruption cycle.', monthlyEvents:[{month:'2026-03',status:'NOTABLE',label:'Q1 2026: 2,505 victims; Qilin #1, DragonForce cartel, encryption-free surge',detail:'Q1 2026: 2,505 victims disclosed on DLS sites. Qilin #1 (30 victims/week peak); Akira #2 ($244M cumulative); LockBit5 rebranded; DragonForce cartel (363 victims, 80% affiliate split). BravoX new entry with selective screening. AtomSilo reactivated. Encryption-free extortion grew from 2% to 22% of IR cases (Nov 2024 to Nov 2025).',conf:'CONFIRMED'},{month:'2026-03',status:'NOTABLE',label:'Ptitsyn (Phobos administrator) pleads guilty; $1.77M forfeiture',detail:'Phobos administrator Evgenii Ptitsyn pleaded guilty in the District of Maryland (Mar 3-4) to wire fraud conspiracy tied to a Phobos operation with 1,000+ victims and roughly $39M extorted ecosystem-wide; forfeits $1.77M; sentencing July 15, 2026 (maximum 20 years). An operator-tier guilty plea with a forfeiture component, against the pattern of RU-nexus administrators remaining beyond reach.',conf:'CONFIRMED'},{month:'2026-04',status:'NOTABLE',label:'Q1 2026: 2,638 victim posts (+22% YoY); Vect mass launch; The Gentlemen 5x actual scale',detail:'Q1 2026: 2,638 victim posts (22% increase vs Q1 2025). 91 active leak sites (record). Vect RaaS distributed affiliate keys to 300K+ BreachForums members (Apr 16); VECT 2.0 wiper bug discovered Apr 28-29. The Gentlemen: 1,570 actual victims confirmed via SystemBC C2 analysis vs 320 published. Qilin: 107 Apr victims, 338 Q1 total (#1).',conf:'CONFIRMED'},{month:'2026-05',status:'NOTABLE',label:'The Gentlemen admin identity exposed; LockBit 5.0 +106%; May volume -9.4% MoM; Karakurt state-nexus precedent',detail:'Four significant May developments. (1) The Gentlemen backend breach (May 4): admin identity zeta88/hastalamuerte exposed via leaked Rocket database; TOX ID F8E24C7F5B12CD69C44C73F438F65E9BF560ADF35EBBDF92CF9A9B84079F8F04060FF98D098E confirmed; full 16.22 GB breach dataset likely contains complete victim records, negotiation logs, and wallet history; 30-60 day enforcement window before operational security rebuild completes. (2) LockBit 5.0: +106% victim count vs Q4 2025; 4th globally Q1 2026 (163 victims); cross-platform (Windows/Linux/ESXi); climbed from outside top 10. (3) May victim volume: ~700 estimated (9.4% MoM decline from 772 confirmed April), directionally consistent with Operation Saffron/Stark Infrastructure disruptions — seasonal variation cannot be excluded. 8 new groups debuted May: Fulcrumsec (23 victims), Leakbazaar (9), Blackwater (6), Cmdorganization (4), others. (4) Karakurt: Deniss Zolotarjovs sentenced 8.5 years (May 6); DOJ confirmed gang used Russian government database access and paid military draft bribes — first judicial establishment of state-criminal nexus model for RU-nexus ransomware. Structural: ransom payment rate collapsed from 76% (2019) to 28% (2026, Kaspersky); 69% of victims refused payment in 2025 (Verizon 2026 DBIR).',conf:'CONFIRMED'},{month:'2026-06',status:'NOTABLE',label:'The Gentlemen operator publicly identified (Yapaev, Izhevsk); jurisdictional-pressure window open',detail:'The operator of the number-two YTD RaaS (478+ cumulative victims across 66 countries by mid-June) was publicly identified by Brian Krebs as Alexander Andreevich Yapaev of Izhevsk, Russia, with affiliate TOX IDs exposed. Go-based, Garble-obfuscated locker with self-propagating worm capability and an anti-recovery wipe routine; 90/10 affiliate split; heavy AI use in development. Pressure window assessed at 60-90 days before OPSEC hardening or rebrand per the Embargo-to-ArmCorp-to-Gentlemen precedent: indictment plus Red Notice while exposure is fresh.',conf:'CREDIBLE'},{month:'2026-06',status:'NOTABLE',label:'Fragmentation deepens: 106 active groups YTD, top-10 share near 50%; June volume ~722 (+12%, provisional)',detail:'2026 YTD: 106 active groups across 130 countries, 4,780 victims, 37 new groups (June debuts include Settra, Blackfield, Deadlock, and Wallstreet). The top-10 share of attacks has fallen to roughly 50% from 69% in 2022. June leak-site volume reached roughly 722 organizations versus 646 in May (+12%, PROVISIONAL: still-accruing tracker count that may reflect deferred May publication). Qilin assessed as the dominant operator for a sixth consecutive month.',conf:'CREDIBLE'}] },
  { id:'affiliate',conf:'CONFIRMED',label:['RaaS','Affiliates'],         module:'M07', nodeRef:'Node: Aff',layer:4, tier:'HIGH',   replace:'MEDIUM', backfire:'MEDIUM',  protected:false, owner:'LE / victim-side',   phase:null, jurisdiction:'western',    desc:'Operational workforce conducting intrusions, deployment, and victim handling.', why:'Revenue calculus most directly affected by non-payment rate growth. Coveware reports greater than 70% non-payment for professionally supported victims versus 30-40% for unsupported victims — the most direct ransomware revenue reduction figure in the EDP dataset. Scaling professional negotiation support degrades affiliate revenue measurably without requiring LE access to Russian infrastructure.', hit:'Decryptors, victim pre-notification, arrests, revenue suppression through non-payment rate growth, professional negotiation support scaling.', damage:'Campaign completion rate and franchise attractiveness decline.', successor:'New affiliates or migration to another brand.', monthlyEvents:[{month:'2026-04',status:'NOTABLE',label:'Vect RaaS: 300K+ affiliate keys distributed via BreachForums (Apr 16)',detail:'Vect RaaS distributed functional affiliate keys to all 300,000+ BreachForums members on April 16 — the largest single-event expansion of the ransomware affiliate pool documented. VECT 2.0 wiper bug (irreversibly destroys files >131KB on Windows/Linux/ESXi) discovered April 28-29, undermining affiliate trust and monetization capacity.',conf:'CONFIRMED'},{month:'2026-04',status:'NOTABLE',label:'Scattered Spider ringleader Buchanan pleads guilty (Apr 20)',detail:'Tyler Buchanan pleaded guilty in the Central District of California to wire fraud conspiracy and aggravated identity theft; in US custody, sentencing August 21, 2026. A custody outcome at the Western-affiliate edge of the ecosystem, where enforcement leverage actually exists.',conf:'CONFIRMED'},{month:'2026-05',status:'NOTABLE',label:'The Gentlemen: 8 affiliate TOX IDs exposed; AI-assisted RaaS dev; dual-leverage extortion tactic',detail:'Check Point Research (May 13) analysis of the May 4 Gentlemen backend breach identified: 8 distinct affiliate TOX IDs across 29 confirmed campaigns; most active affiliate (TOX 98C132E2...) ran 11 of 29 campaigns; 90/10 split model (90% to affiliate); approximately 9 named operators in leaked communications. Admin zeta88 plays dual role: manages platform, builds locker, runs payouts, AND directly conducts infections. AI-assisted development confirmed: GLOCKER RaaS panel built in 3 days using DeepSeek, Qwen, and Kimi — first documented operational preference for Chinese LLMs over Western models in RaaS development. Operators discussing self-hosted uncensored LLMs for automated triage of exfiltrated victim data (planned, not yet deployed). Novel dual-leverage tactic documented: stolen data from Victim A reused to attack Victim B, then Victim A publicly attributed as access broker on DLS — cascading extortion pressure at zero additional intrusion cost.',conf:'CONFIRMED'},{month:'2026-05',status:'NOTABLE',label:'LockBit affiliate sentenced to 102 months',detail:'A Latvian LockBit affiliate was sentenced to 102 months in US federal court in May 2026. Single-source reporting.',conf:'CREDIBLE'},{month:'2026-06',status:'NOTABLE',label:'Jubair and Flowers plead guilty on day one of UK trial (Jun 26)',detail:'Thalha Jubair and Owen Flowers pleaded guilty at Woolwich Crown Court on the first day of trial over the Transport for London attack; Flowers also admitted US healthcare intrusions. Both await UK sentencing; Jubair faces outstanding US charges. Continues the pattern of custody outcomes concentrating at the Western-affiliate edge.',conf:'CONFIRMED'},{month:'2026-07',status:'NOTABLE',label:'Vardanyan (Ryuk deployer) pleads guilty (Jul 8); Scattered Spider suspect Stokes extradited (Jul 1)',detail:'Karen Vardanyan, extradited from Ukraine in 2025, pleaded guilty July 8 to conspiracy and computer fraud counts (up to 15 years); sentencing September 22, 2026. Tied to 2,400+ attacks and more than $15M in BTC extorted November 2019 to April 2020; custody achieved via the Ukraine extradition channel, with no current ecosystem effect since the Ryuk lineage is defunct. Separately, Scattered Spider suspect Peter Stokes was extradited from Finland July 1 and detained pending trial (roughly $8M extortion attempt, 100+ intrusions).',conf:'CONFIRMED'}] },
  { id:'datamkt', conf:'CONFIRMED',  label:['Underground','Data Markets'], module:'M16',  nodeRef:'Node 06b', layer:5, tier:'MEDIUM', replace:'HIGH',   backfire:'LOW',     protected:false, owner:'LE / forum disruption', phase:'B', jurisdiction:'western',  desc:'Criminal forums and marketplaces where stolen data is auctioned or sold when a victim refuses to pay ransom — the primary monetization fallback after failed double-extortion.', why:'Underground data markets (BreachForums, RAMP, Exploit.in, and successors) function as the financial fallback that sustains the double-extortion model even when ransom is not paid. Without a functional sales channel, non-payment becomes a pure cost to the actor. Forum seizures that eliminate the data sales infrastructure — not just the leak site — reduce the marginal value of each intrusion and shift victim incentives toward non-payment.', hit:'Forum seizure, escrow disruption, trust degradation, buyer-side sting operations, data buyer sanctions, takedowns timed to eliminate the sales channel simultaneously with leak site.', damage:'Non-payment by victims becomes financially viable — actors cannot recover sunk intrusion costs through data sales.', successor:'New forums, Telegram-based sales channels, direct buyer outreach.', monthlyEvents:[{month:'2026-03',status:'DISRUPTED',label:'LeakBase taken down — Operation Leak (Mar 3-4)',detail:'LeakBase — one of the world largest credential trading forums — dismantled by FBI/Europol. 142K members, 215K+ messages, hundreds of millions of credentials captured. Alternative platforms (XSS, Exploit.in, Genesis successor markets) absorbing displaced user base within 30-90 days.',conf:'CONFIRMED'}] },
  { id:'leaksite', conf:'CONFIRMED', label:['Leak Site','Operations'],    module:'M08', nodeRef:'Node 06', layer:5, tier:'HIGH',    replace:'MEDIUM', backfire:'LOW',     protected:false, owner:'LE / infra',         phase:'C', jurisdiction:'western',    desc:'Double-extortion publication infrastructure used to coerce payment.', why:'Enforcement mechanism of double extortion. Without functional DLS infrastructure, ransomware degrades to single extortion with historically lower payment rates. The Hive covert-access model — 7 months of FBI access before seizure, 300+ victims notified, $130M in avoided payments — is the highest-impact DLS approach and has not been replicated at scale.', hit:'Hosting seizure, BPH backbone disruption, trust destruction, covert access before public seizure, decryptor timing.', damage:'Payment coercion weakens and victim refusal becomes more likely.', successor:'Fresh leak sites, mirrors, new hosting. Multi-tenant DLS model means one backbone disruption simultaneously takes down multiple brands.', monthlyEvents:[{month:'2026-04',status:'NOTABLE',label:'Record 91 active leak sites in Q1 2026 (vs ~50 mid-2024)',detail:'Q1 2026: 91 active data-disclosure sites (record; vs ~50 mid-2024). Post-RAMP fragmentation driving proliferation rather than contraction — disruption of central coordination nodes is accelerating distributed DLS infrastructure. 2,638 victim posts Q1 2026, 22% increase vs Q1 2025.',conf:'CONFIRMED'},{month:'2026-05',status:'NOTABLE',label:'May ~700 victims est. (-9.4% MoM); 8 new groups debuted; RAMP 120+ days dark',detail:'May 2026 estimated ~700 victims (9.4% MoM decline from 772 confirmed in April; Source: Ransomware.live tracking — final count will update at month close). Decline directionally consistent with Operation Saffron/Stark infrastructure disruptions; seasonal variation and DLS publishing lag cannot be excluded. Cannot confirm whether groups are publishing fewer victims or publishing slower — analytically significant for ransom leverage assessment. 8 new groups debuted May: Fulcrumsec (23 victims), Leakbazaar (9), Blackwater (6), Cmdorganization (4), Prinzeugen (1), Icarus (1), Shadowbyt3$ (reactivated), Titan. YTD 2026: approximately 3,894 victims across 97 active groups and 119 countries. RAMP non-reconstitution at 120+ days is the single most anomalous structural signal: competing hypothesis is permanent private-channel migration, which reduces open-source DLS visibility.',conf:'CONFIRMED'},{month:'2026-06',status:'NOTABLE',label:'Data-only extortion consolidates: World Leaks, SRG, and ShinyHunters all encryption-free in June; ~22% of IR cases',detail:'World Leaks (Hunters International rebrand) listed Tata Electronics, an Apple contract manufacturer: 204,341 files / 630.4 GB (confirmed by BleepingComputer Jun 23). Silent Ransom Group reached roughly 100 listed victims, compressing end-to-end extortion to under one business day via vishing and in-person USB theft, with leak infrastructure on an 18-country, 22-ISP residential botnet to resist takedown (Mandiant/Resecurity). Best-available baseline: 22% of incident-response cases now run data-only, up roughly 11x year over year (primary source unverified).',conf:'CREDIBLE'}] },
  { id:'negot',    conf:'CONFIRMED',    label:['Negotiation','Services'],    module:'M15', nodeRef:'No node', layer:5, tier:'MEDIUM',  replace:'MEDIUM', backfire:'LOW',     protected:false, owner:'Victim-side pressure', phase:null, jurisdiction:'western',   desc:'Monetization bridge managing final ransom negotiation and payment terms.', why:'Analytically distinct: legitimate negotiation services are a defender-side countermeasure, not adversary infrastructure. Scaling access to professional negotiation is the highest-ROI non-LE disruption action in the ecosystem. The non-payment rate gap between supported victims (greater than 70%) and unsupported victims (30-40%) is the largest unaddressed disruption opportunity.', hit:'Victim guidance, response hardening, mandatory disclosure standards, OFAC screening requirements for commercial negotiation firms.', damage:'Settlement efficiency falls and negotiations fail more often.', successor:'Operators absorb the role directly, accepting lower conversion rates.', monthlyEvents:[{month:'2026-04',status:'NOTABLE',label:'Insider-compromise vector documented: IR and negotiation insiders sentenced as ALPHV affiliates (Apr 22)',detail:'Ryan Goldberg and Kevin Martin, US incident-response and negotiation professionals who moonlighted as ALPHV/BlackCat affiliates, were sentenced April 22; Angelo Martino pleaded guilty April 20 (sentencing July 9, 2026). The case documents an insider-compromise vector inside the victim-side negotiation layer: professionals with privileged access to victim negotiations operating as affiliates of the group across the table. Implication for the collect-only posture: the layer is a compromise surface as well as an intelligence source, and commercial negotiation firms require vetting and OFAC screening.',conf:'CONFIRMED'}] },
  { id:'bridge',  conf:'CREDIBLE',    label:['Cross-Chain','Bridges'],      module:'M11b', nodeRef:'Node 08b', layer:6, tier:'MEDIUM', replace:'MEDIUM', backfire:'LOW',     protected:false, owner:'OFAC / blockchain analytics', phase:'A', jurisdiction:'western',  desc:'Decentralized or semi-decentralized protocols enabling cryptocurrency conversion across blockchains (BTC to ETH, USDT chain-hopping) to break on-chain tracing continuity.', why:'Cross-chain bridges are better documented in DeFi exploit laundering than in mainstream ransomware cash-out, but adoption is increasing as mixer enforcement narrows alternatives. Thorchain, RenBTC, and similar protocols allow actors to move value between chains without a centralized exchange creating a KYC choke point. OFAC has begun extending designation to bridge components. Confidence is CREDIBLE — the technique is confirmed but its prevalence in the ransomware stack specifically remains an emerging pattern.', hit:'OFAC designation of bridge smart contracts and associated wallets, blockchain analytics flagging of cross-chain hops, exchange-level screening of funds with bridge provenance.', damage:'On-chain tracing is restored; actors must use higher-friction alternatives.', successor:'New bridge protocols, privacy coins (Monero), multi-hop DEX routing.', monthlyEvents:[] },
  { id:'mixers',   conf:'CONFIRMED',   label:['Crypto','Mixers'],           module:'M11', nodeRef:'Node 08', layer:6, tier:'HIGH',    replace:'MEDIUM', backfire:'LOW',     protected:false, owner:'OFAC / tracing',     phase:'B', jurisdiction:'western',    desc:'Financial obfuscation layer breaking transaction visibility before cash-out.', why:'The 35% decline in ransomware payments in 2024 occurred during the most active mixer enforcement period in the asset class history. Mixer disruption reduces ROI per attack, the most structurally durable deterrence form available. Attacker adaptation toward multi-service layering stacks is the current challenge.', hit:'Sanctions, tracing, exchange-level flagging, OFAC designation extended to cross-chain bridge and DEX components.', damage:'Funds move onto higher-friction rails and become easier to trace.', successor:'Other mixers, chain-hopping, privacy tools, multi-service layering stacks.', monthlyEvents:[{month:'2026-06',status:'DISRUPTED',label:'AudiA6 laundering service and Dark2Web forum dismantled (Jun 10): EUR 336M laundered, 2 admins arrested',detail:'Europol, US Secret Service, and IRS-CI with 10 countries dismantled the AudiA6 mixer-as-a-service: roughly EUR 336M laundered since 2021 through 6,000+ KYC money-mule accounts; 393.39 BTC (about $19.2M) traced illicit within roughly 10,333 BTC deposited; about 80% of traced illicit exposure was ransomware across 20+ groups. Administrators Ruslan Tkachuk and Alexander Ledenev arrested in Batumi, Georgia; 25 domains, 30+ servers, and 80+ vehicles seized; the linked Dark2Web forum taken down. Tied to 15+ ransomware and crypto-theft investigations.',conf:'CONFIRMED'}] },
  { id:'otc',      conf:'CONFIRMED',      label:['OTC Crypto','Brokers'],      module:'M12', nodeRef:'Node 01', layer:7, tier:'CRITICAL',replace:'HIGH',   backfire:'LOW',     protected:false, owner:'Treasury / OFAC',    phase:'A', jurisdiction:'western',    desc:'Large-volume off-exchange brokers converting crypto to fiat at scale.', why:'Highest-priority Phase A financial target. The SUEX-to-Garantex-to-Cryptex designation sequence is the proof of concept for the EDP Phase A playbook. The April 2025 Garantex shutdown created the largest ruble-liquidity gap since SUEX in 2021. T3 Financial Crime Unit USDT blacklisting capability operates faster than formal designation and should be institutionalized.', hit:'Designation, VASP and KYC pressure, correspondent banking exposure, T3 Financial Crime Unit USDT blacklisting, coordinated simultaneous action with exchange designation.', damage:'High-value ransom revenue becomes stranded or harder to liquidate.', successor:'Smaller brokers, fragmented cash-out.', monthlyEvents:[] },
  { id:'cex',     conf:'CREDIBLE',    label:['Compliant','Exchange (Nested)'], module:'M13b', nodeRef:'Node 02b', layer:7, tier:'MEDIUM', replace:'LOW',    backfire:'LOW',     protected:false, owner:'FinCEN / Regulators',  phase:'A', jurisdiction:'western',   desc:'Regulated exchanges used as unwitting conduits through nested account abuse by non-compliant intermediaries and OTC desks.', why:'Compliant CEX share of ransomware off-ramping rose to 39% even as total payments fell — the risk is not at the compliant exchange itself but in the nested OTC desk and straw-man account supply chain that sits inside it. The leverage point is forcing compliant exchanges to apply enhanced due diligence to their highest-risk correspondent relationships.', hit:'SAR filing obligations, enhanced due diligence on nested accounts, debanking of high-risk correspondent exchange relationships, FinCEN 314(b) information sharing, BSA/AML enforcement against compliance failures.', damage:'Nested intermediary accounts frozen; non-compliant exchanges lose their clean off-ramp.', successor:'Private OTC desks operating outside regulated perimeter.', monthlyEvents:[{month:'2026-03',status:'DISRUPTED',label:'UK sanctioned Xinbi marketplace — $19.9B in transactions (Mar 26)',detail:'UK became first country globally to sanction Xinbi cryptocurrency marketplace (processed $19.9B in transactions 2021-2025). FBI and Thai police simultaneously froze $580M in crypto linked to Southeast Asian scam networks targeting US nationals.',conf:'CONFIRMED'},{month:'2026-04',status:'NOTABLE',label:'OFAC: 29 Cambodian entities designated; $701.9M restrained (Apr 23)',detail:'OFAC designated 29 Cambodian entities (Senator Kok An network) for money laundering via scam center operations. $701.9M in cryptocurrency restrained; 503 websites seized. EU 20th sanctions package banned all Russian/Belarusian crypto platforms from EU-nexus transactions, effective May 24, 2026.',conf:'CONFIRMED'},{month:'2026-05',status:'NOTABLE',label:'OFAC sanctions Sinaloa Cartel fentanyl-to-crypto network (May 20); EU Russian crypto ban takes effect',detail:'OFAC (May 20, 2026) sanctioned a Sinaloa Cartel fentanyl-to-crypto laundering network (Los Chapitos faction; Armando de Jesus Ojeda Aviles network): 11 individuals, 2 entities designated for converting U.S. drug proceeds to cryptocurrency and moving funds to Mexico. Financial cell disrupted; cartel retains redundant laundering infrastructure. EU 20th sanctions package effective May 24: total ban on Russian and Belarusian crypto platform transactions with EU-nexus entities; digital ruble (CBDC), RUBx stablecoin, and TengriCoin also banned. Tether (USDT) cooperation signal: April 24, OFAC + Tether froze $344M USDT linked to Central Bank of Iran-affiliated wallets — the largest single crypto freeze tied to a state-nexus actor and a direct demonstration of Tether\'s willingness to cooperate with OFAC at scale.',conf:'CONFIRMED'},{month:'2026-06',status:'NOTABLE',label:'OFAC designates four Iranian exchanges (Jun 2): exchange-level chokepoint lever extended beyond Russia/CIS',detail:'Nobitex (roughly 50% of Iranian digital-asset inflows), Wallex, Bitpin, and Ramzinex designated along with four Nobitex executives, for sanctions evasion and IRGC-linked ransomware facilitation; the Iranian ecosystem processed over $7.78B in 2025. Roughly $500M in regime-linked crypto frozen (Chainalysis/OFAC). Largest US action to date against the Iranian digital-asset sector; replicates the Garantex-model exchange chokepoint in a second jurisdiction.',conf:'CONFIRMED'}] },
  { id:'ruexch',  conf:'CONFIRMED',   label:['Russia-Based','Exchanges'],  module:'M13c', nodeRef:'Node 02c', layer:7, tier:'HIGH',    replace:'MEDIUM', backfire:'LOW-MED', protected:false, owner:'OFAC / Treasury / FinCEN', phase:'A', jurisdiction:'domestic', desc:'Russian cryptocurrency exchanges — including those purporting to operate from Estonia, Georgia, or other jurisdictions — serving as primary high-volume ruble conversion and laundering infrastructure.', why:'Garantex, SUEX, Cryptex, and Bitzlato represent a documented sequence of Russian exchange designations, each displacing volume to the next until physical enforcement closed the gap. The pattern shows designation alone produces 6-18 months of disruption before reconstitution. Exchanges registered in Estonia or Georgia with Russian beneficial ownership and operations are a specific subcategory: enforcement jurisdiction is formally accessible but politically complicated. Physical enforcement within 12 months of designation is the difference between temporary friction and structural damage.', hit:'OFAC designation, FinCEN 311 action, physical enforcement coordinated with host-jurisdiction partners (Europol, Estonia PPA, Georgia LEPL), beneficial ownership exposure, correspondent banking isolation.', damage:'Ruble liquidity gap forces volume onto smaller, less liquid brokers — higher cost and slower settlement for criminal actors.', successor:'Smaller Russian OTC desks, fragmented cash-out through informal hawala-style networks.', monthlyEvents:[{month:'2026-04',status:'DISRUPTED',label:'Grinex (Garantex successor) hacked and suspended — $13.7M lost (Apr 15)',detail:'Grinex exchange — OFAC-designated Garantex successor — hacked April 15; $13.7M lost; operations suspended. Primary Russia-linked ransomware money-laundering node eliminated. Displaced volume expected to migrate to OTC brokers and non-designated Central Asian exchanges within days.',conf:'CONFIRMED'},{month:'2026-04',status:'DEGRADED',label:'EU 20th sanctions: total ban on Russian crypto platforms, effective May 24',detail:'EU 20th sanctions package (Apr 23) imposed total ban on Russian and Belarusian crypto platforms, digital ruble (CBDC), RUBx stablecoin, and TengriCoin. Effective May 24, 2026. Russia A7A5 ruble-backed token processed estimated $93.3B in sanctions evasion. 31-day window before displacement consolidates into a new primary laundering node.',conf:'CONFIRMED'},{month:'2026-05',status:'NOTABLE',label:'OFAC delists 76 SDN entries (May 28): no ecosystem targets among removals',detail:'OFAC removed 76 SDN entries in a sanctions-modernization delisting action. No ransomware, exchange, or BPH targets were among the removals: a negative-confirmation datapoint that the Garantex-lineage and wider ecosystem designations all remain in force.',conf:'CONFIRMED'}] },
  { id:'exchl',    conf:'CONFIRMED',    label:['Non-Compliant','Exchanges'], module:'M13', nodeRef:'Node 02', layer:7, tier:'CRITICAL',replace:'HIGH',   backfire:'LOW',     protected:false, owner:'Regulators / Treasury',phase:'A', jurisdiction:'western',   desc:'High-risk exchanges and laundering services bridging crypto into spendable fiat.', why:'Compliant CEX share of ransomware off-ramping rose to 39% even as total payments fell 35% — enforcement is working correctly. The leverage point is the intermediary account supply chain (nested OTC desks, straw-man account suppliers), not exchanges themselves. Designation alone is insufficient for RU-domiciled exchanges: physical enforcement must follow within 12-18 months.', hit:'Designation, enhanced due diligence referrals, KYC pressure, correspondent banking de-risking, physical enforcement follow-up within 12-18 months of designation.', damage:'Crypto remains trapped or routes through narrower alternatives.', successor:'Secondary exchanges and OTC fallback.', monthlyEvents:[{month:'2026-05',status:'DEGRADED',label:'First UK use of Regulation 17A: HTX, Exmo, Rapira, Bitpapa designated (May 26)',detail:'First UK use of Regulation 17A against cryptoasset exchanges, extending correspondent-banking-style prohibitions to crypto exchanges for the first time. 18 targets in the A7 network package: HTX (Huobi Global, the first UK designation of a major global exchange, accused of channeling $1.5B+ to Russia), Exmo Exchange Ltd, Rapira Group, Bitpapa IC FZC, Nueva Cryptologia (ABCEX alias), and Eurasian Savings Bank plus 8 more entities and 4 individuals. The A7 network is claimed to move roughly $90B into the Russian economy. The designated venues are the named absorbers of displaced Garantex/Grinex flow; the settlement rail they ride on is tracked at the A7A5 node.',conf:'CONFIRMED'}] },
  { id:'mules',    conf:'CREDIBLE',    label:['Mule','Networks'],           module:'M14', nodeRef:'Node 09', layer:8, tier:'HIGH',    replace:'MEDIUM', backfire:'LOW-MED', protected:false, owner:'LE / FNS / Rosfin',  phase:'C', jurisdiction:'domestic',   desc:'Fiat layering and integration network moving money into the legitimate economy.', why:'The structural leverage point is velocity, not individual accounts. Greater than 50% of mule-routed funds exit within one hour. Real-time payment rail intervention is the only mechanism that can disrupt the layering function at scale. Herder-tier prosecution produces disruption value across the entire cybercrime ecosystem, not just ransomware.', hit:'Recruitment disruption, herder-tier prosecution, laundering cases, domestic financial pressure via FNS and Rosfinmonitoring, real-time payment network mule detection.', damage:'Spendability and real-economy integration slow sharply.', successor:'New mule recruiters and alternate fronts.', monthlyEvents:[{month:'2026-06',status:'NOTABLE',label:'AudiA6 takedown exposes a 6,000+ KYC mule-account network',detail:'The June 10 AudiA6 action documented a laundering model built on 6,000+ verified money-mule accounts feeding cash-out, confirming the mule-network layer as the KYC bypass mechanism for exchange-based laundering at scale.',conf:'CONFIRMED'}] },
  { id:'bankacct', conf:'CONFIRMED',  label:['Bank Account','Transfer'],    module:'M17',  nodeRef:'Node 09b', layer:8, tier:'MEDIUM', replace:'MEDIUM', backfire:'LOW',     protected:false, owner:'FinCEN / LE / correspondent banking', phase:'A', jurisdiction:'western',  desc:'Final fiat integration point where laundered proceeds enter the legitimate banking system through wire transfers, cash deposits, or real estate and business investment.', why:'Bank account seizure is a distinct enforcement tool from exchange sanctions — faster to execute, requires lower evidentiary threshold, and directly freezes spendable proceeds. The leverage point is correspondent banking: Western banks applying de-risking pressure to Russian and offshore banks that service criminal-linked accounts create friction without requiring direct attribution to specific actors.', hit:'Wire transfer interdiction, BSA/SAR referrals, correspondent banking de-risking, asset forfeiture, real estate beneficial ownership disclosure requirements, FinCEN 314(a) and 314(b) information sharing.', damage:'Proceeds cannot enter the legitimate economy — criminal actors hold illiquid crypto or stranded ruble accounts.', successor:'Informal value transfer, hawala networks, real estate in permissive jurisdictions.', monthlyEvents:[] },
  { id:'a7a5',     conf:'CONFIRMED',  label:['A7A5 Ruble','Settlement Rail'], module:'M13c', nodeRef:'Node 02d', layer:8, tier:'CRITICAL', replace:'HIGH', backfire:'MEDIUM', protected:true, owner:'OFAC / EU / UK / Treasury', phase:'A', jurisdiction:'domestic', desc:'Ruble-backed settlement token (A7A5) issued by A7 LLC, a Moscow cross-border payments firm co-owned by sanctioned financier Ilan Shor (51%) and the sanctioned, defense-linked Promsvyazbank (49%). The settlement layer the post-Garantex exchange network rides on.', why:'The chokepoint that did not move. Exchange front-ends churned (Garantex seized March 2025, successor Grinex dark April 2026, trading concentrated onto Meer and Rapira) while the settlement rail underneath persisted through every rebrand: A7A5 carried Garantex customer balances into Grinex, cleared roughly $93B in its first year, and has since passed $100B in reported turnover (Confirmed). In October 2025 the token was approved for Russian foreign-trade settlement, with its operators openly targeting a fifth of the country\'s international settlements; the UK describes the A7 network as moving more than $90B into the Russian economy and financing procurement for the war (Confirmed). The rail has absorbed designations from three jurisdictions (OFAC August 2025 naming the A7 settlement network, EU October 2025 full token ban, UK May 2026 A7 network package) and kept clearing. Exchange brands are interchangeable front-ends; the rail is banking access, correspondent relationships, and state alignment, none of which a rebrand replaces. Seizing a domain interrupts a venue; reaching the settlement rail interrupts the function (Analyst inference).', hit:'Designation of the token, issuer, and settlement network (OFAC named A7, A71, A7 Agent, Old Vector, InDeFi Bank, and Exved in August 2025), designation at birth of successor venues carrying the rail (Meer, EU April 2026), correspondent isolation of Promsvyazbank-linked settlement, issuer-level stablecoin obligations on the GENIUS Act model, and Travel Rule enforcement at the venues within reach.', damage:'The post-Garantex exchange network loses its settlement floor: ruble conversion falls back to slower, thinner channels with visible banking touchpoints, and displaced venue volume cannot rebrand its way back onto a functioning rail.', successor:'No successor rail of comparable scale is documented. Alternative ruble instruments (RUBx stablecoin, digital ruble) are EU-banned since May 2026; the fallback is direct settlement through sanctioned banks or informal value transfer.', monthlyEvents:[{month:'2026-04',status:'NOTABLE',label:'Grinex goes dark; the rail persists, trading concentrates onto Meer (Meer.kg)',detail:'Grinex, the OFAC-designated Garantex successor, reported a roughly $13.7M theft (about 1 billion rubles) on April 15, blamed foreign special services, and suspended operations; on-chain analysis reads the outflows as consistent with self-directed laundering, and the exit-scam hypothesis is credible and unresolved. A7A5 trading did not stop with the venue: it concentrated onto Meer (Meer.kg), the Kyrgyz exchange run by CJSC TengriCoin that had handled the token in parallel and became its primary venue after the suspension. What moved was the venue carrying the rail, not the rail itself.',conf:'CREDIBLE'},{month:'2026-04',status:'DEGRADED',label:'EU 20th package designates Meer operator TengriCoin; full RU/BY crypto-platform ban (Apr 23)',detail:'The EU 20th sanctions package designated CJSC TengriCoin (operator of Meer.kg, the primary post-Grinex A7A5 venue) and banned the RUBx stablecoin and the digital ruble, alongside a full ban on transactions with all Russia- and Belarus-based crypto service providers effective May 24, 2026. The package targets the post-Garantex evasion architecture at the venue layer while the settlement rail continues to clear.',conf:'CONFIRMED'},{month:'2026-05',status:'NOTABLE',label:'A7A5 token: $72B illicit flows; 95% of ransomware proceeds via USDT stablecoins; EU ban active',detail:'TRM Labs 2026 Crypto Crime Report: Russia-linked sanctioned flows totaled $93 billion in 2025; the A7A5 token cluster alone processed $72 billion. 95% of illicit ransomware-related inflows to sanctioned entities/jurisdictions transited stablecoins in 2025, primarily USDT. This concentration makes Tether Limited the single highest-leverage financial chokepoint available to law enforcement: the entire ecosystem payment collection chain depends on USDT access, Tether operates in a Western-accessible jurisdiction, and existing BSA/AML frameworks already apply (unenforced at wallet-screening level). EU ban on Russian crypto platforms (from 20th sanctions package) came into effect May 24, 2026. Structural leverage action recommended: OFAC designation of A7A5 token under 31 CFR Part 510; Treasury guidance requiring Tether to implement SDN-list wallet screening under penalty of USD correspondent banking access loss. Window: 12-18 months before alternative payment rails mature.',conf:'CONFIRMED'},{month:'2026-05',status:'DEGRADED',label:'UK A7 network package: 18 designations under Regulation 17A (May 26)',detail:'First UK use of Regulation 17A against cryptoasset exchanges, aimed squarely at the A7 network: HTX, Exmo, Rapira, Bitpapa, Nueva Cryptologia (ABCEX alias), and Eurasian Savings Bank plus 8 more entities and 4 individuals including Sergey Mendeleev. The UK claims the network moves roughly $90B. Third jurisdiction to designate the rail and its venues; the settlement function continued operating, which is the measure that matters.',conf:'CONFIRMED'}] },

  { id:'fsb',   conf:'ANALYST',   label:['FSB','Protection'],  module:'RU', nodeRef:'FSB',    layer:'state', tier:'HIGH', replace:'LOW', backfire:'HIGH',    protected:true,  owner:'State actor',          phase:null, jurisdiction:null, desc:'Primary architect of protection, recruitment, and shielding relationships.', role:'Architect of Russia cybercrime protection through the krysha model: transactional protection relationships between FSB officers and criminal actors. Not a unified institution — competing internal factions with divergent financial interests are exploitable.', lever:'Exploit FSB internal factional competition. Officer financial exposure (lifestyle vs. declared salary) surfaced to competing units via FNS and Rosfinmonitoring creates domestic liability FSB cannot easily shield.', targets:'Officers maintaining krysha relationships with high-volume ransomware actors. Mid-tier actors whose protection cost exceeds their strategic value to FSB.', constraints:'Direct pressure invariably activates protection: public attribution, extradition requests, and media naming reliably convert actors into protected state assets. FSB absorption is not reversible through additional pressure.', success:'Protected actor becomes a domestic liability. Protecting officer faces competing FSB faction scrutiny. FSB distances from the actor rather than reinforcing protection.', monthlyEvents:[{month:'2026-06',status:'NOTABLE',label:'CIS-exclusion actively enforced: Nova crew bans affiliate over Uzbek/Moscow-linked victim',detail:'The Nova crew (an affiliate program of the RAlord operation) banned an affiliate in early June for hitting Eriell Group, an oilfield-services firm headquartered in Uzbekistan with Moscow offices, then issued a public apology, pledged no leak, and offered free recovery. Textbook safe-harbor self-policing: avoiding domestic victims that would trigger Russian law enforcement. Rare direct observational evidence of the protection-layer rule set operating in real time.',conf:'CREDIBLE'}] },
  { id:'mvd',   conf:'CONFIRMED',   label:['MVD','Dept K'],      module:'RU', nodeRef:'MVD',    layer:'state', tier:'HIGH', replace:'LOW', backfire:'MEDIUM',  protected:false, owner:'Russian domestic LE',   phase:null, jurisdiction:null, desc:'Most actionable domestic enforcement lever for mid-tier actors outside the protected core.', role:'Primary domestic cyber enforcement body. Sits below FSB in the hierarchy — FSB can override any case with national security framing. For actors without confirmed FSB affiliation, MVD is responsive, quota-driven, and reachable through structured intelligence packages.', lever:'Structured intelligence packages via FBI and NCA liaisons, framed as laundering and tax evasion rather than cybercrime. FNS and Rosfinmonitoring referrals feed MVD without triggering FSB review.', targets:'Bulletproof hosting operators, initial access brokers, OTC money launderers, and mule networks all sit in MVD enforcement lane without FSB override in most cases.', constraints:'FSB override is automatic when FSB-adjacent actor names appear in MVD referral packages. Post-2022 enforcement tends toward lenient sentences. Measure forum fragmentation and reconstitution delay, not conviction rates.', success:'Forum fragmentation, affiliate panic, reconstitution delay, and operational tempo decline. Secondary effects are the metric.', monthlyEvents:[] },
  { id:'rosfin',conf:'CONFIRMED',label:['Rosfin','Monitoring'],module:'RU',nodeRef:'Rosfin', layer:'state', tier:'HIGH', replace:'LOW', backfire:'LOW-MED', protected:false, owner:'Russian FIU',            phase:null, jurisdiction:null, desc:'Financial intelligence mapping node linking suspicious crypto-fiat flows to downstream friction.', role:'Russia Financial Intelligence Unit. A mapping and routing node, not an enforcement body. Connects blockchain analytics to domestic Russian enforcement: Rosfinmonitoring flag to CBR 115-FZ bank freeze to MVD referral. Egmont Group membership suspended December 2022.', lever:'Route BTC-to-ruble blockchain intelligence through FinCEN and OFAC VASP actions that force domestic 115-FZ reporting. Use Belarus FIU as primary back-channel post-Egmont suspension.', targets:'OTC broker networks at the crypto-ruble intersection. Post-2024 VASP regulations expanded mandatory reporting significantly.', constraints:'Egmont suspension eliminates direct Western FIU exchange. Belarus FIU back-channel is fragile — one referral that exposes Western sourcing kills it. Selectively responsive for cases the Kremlin wants pursued.', success:'OTC broker accounts flagged under 115-FZ, feeding CBR freeze pipeline. Ruble inflow disruption measurable through OTC reconstitution timelines.', monthlyEvents:[] },
  { id:'cbr',   conf:'CREDIBLE',   label:['CBR','115-FZ'],      module:'RU', nodeRef:'CBR',    layer:'state', tier:'HIGH', replace:'LOW', backfire:'LOW',     protected:false, owner:'Central Bank friction',  phase:null, jurisdiction:null, desc:'Stealth banking friction that can freeze suspicious transactions without public naming.', role:'Non-attributable financial friction mechanism. Can freeze suspicious ruble accounts via 115-FZ banking controls without court orders, public naming, or FSB triggers. Acts on Rosfinmonitoring flags and correspondent banking pressure.', lever:'OFAC designation of OTC brokers triggers de-risking by Western correspondent banks, incentivizing CBR AML compliance without direct engagement. Post-2022 sanctions environment heightens CBR scrutiny.', targets:'Mid-tier and infrastructure actors. OTC broker ruble conversion accounts. Post-2022 VASP transaction flows into Russian-regulated counterparties. CBR will not enforce against politically protected actors.', constraints:'CBR generates no intelligence independently. Correspondent banking pressure takes time to produce friction; build the pipeline early. Will not enforce against FSB or GRU-protected actors.', success:'Actor cannot access ruble proceeds without public naming or FSB trigger. Ruble conversion accounts frozen via 115-FZ without diplomatic exposure.', monthlyEvents:[{month:'2026-04',status:'DEGRADED',label:'EU banned digital ruble and RUBx stablecoin — effective May 24',detail:'EU 20th sanctions package (Apr 23) banned Russia digital ruble (CBDC) and RUBx stablecoin from EU-nexus transactions, effective May 24, 2026. First EU regulatory constraint directly targeting Russia central bank digital currency infrastructure.',conf:'CONFIRMED'}] },
  { id:'gru',     conf:'CREDIBLE',  label:['GRU','Mil. Intel'],    module:'RU', nodeRef:'GRU',     layer:'state', tier:'HIGH',   replace:'LOW', backfire:'HIGH',   protected:true,  owner:'State actor',            phase:null, jurisdiction:null,
    desc:'Military Intelligence Directorate. Distinct from FSB in protection model: GRU relationships tend to be operational rather than passive tolerance. Relevant for identifying actors with strategic tasking overlap.',
    role:'GRU relationships with criminal actors are operational rather than protective. Actors may be used for intelligence collection or strategic destructive effect rather than simply permitted to operate. NotPetya (2017) demonstrated GRU willingness to weaponise criminal-adjacent infrastructure for state ends. Some ransomware actors show targeting patterns consistent with GRU tasking overlap.',
    lever:'Mandatory Dark Covenant 3.0 screening before any pressure action. Identify GRU-adjacent actors and isolate them from campaigns targeting commercially-motivated actors. GRU adjacency is a hard stop on public attribution or designation.',
    targets:'Actors whose targeting patterns show strategic rather than purely financial motivation. Sandworm-adjacent infrastructure. Hosting and VPN providers with documented dual-use relationships.',
    constraints:'Direct pressure activates GRU protective response faster than FSB in most documented cases. Attribution to GRU adjacency carries diplomatic and intelligence costs disproportionate to ransomware disruption value. No domestic enforcement lever is available against GRU-affiliated actors.',
    success:'GRU-adjacent actors correctly identified and excluded from ransomware pressure campaigns. Analyst distinguishes operational GRU relationships from passive FSB krysha before sequencing any action.',
    monthlyEvents:[{month:'2026-07',status:'NOTABLE',label:'EU and UK designate GRU Unit 29155-linked individuals and LLC Impuls (Jul 13)',detail:'The joint EU/UK cyber package reached the state lane directly: the EU designated LLC Impuls and owner Bashev (GRU Unit 29155) and Kasyanenko (GRU SSD, under the destabilising-activities regime); the UK listed 24 individuals and entities, its largest coordinated cyber action to date, including GRU officers Stafeyev, Senin, and Kasyanenko and Unit 29155-linked Shepelev, Puntus, Voronov, and Omarov. Announced with joint UK and EU attribution of the failed attack on the Polish energy grid to FSB Centre 16. Asset freezes plus travel bans; all designated remain at large.',conf:'CONFIRMED'}] },

  { id:'svr',     conf:'CONFIRMED', label:['SVR','Foreign Intel'],  module:'RU', nodeRef:'SVR',     layer:'state', tier:'MEDIUM', replace:'LOW', backfire:'HIGH',   protected:true,  owner:'State actor',            phase:null, jurisdiction:null,
    desc:'Foreign Intelligence Service. Primary focus is espionage, not ransomware enabling. Relevant for correctly scoping the state ecosystem and distinguishing SVR-adjacent APT actors from commercially-motivated ransomware operators.',
    role:'SVR is an espionage service, not a criminal enabler. Its direct relevance to ransomware is limited but important for exclusion logic: SVR-adjacent actors (principally Cozy Bear / APT29) operate under entirely different mandates and political cover than FSB-krysha ransomware actors. Conflating the two produces systematic analytical errors and backfire risk.',
    lever:'Correctly distinguish SVR-adjacent actors from FSB-krysha ransomware actors before any action. SVR adjacency is a full stop on designation, attribution, and public action. Use SVR boundary identification to sharpen targeting of commercially-protected actors.',
    targets:'SVR-adjacent infrastructure where it demonstrably overlaps with criminal ecosystem hosting or VPN providers. Rare. Verify before any action.',
    constraints:'SVR adjacency is the highest political-sensitivity attribution in this ecosystem. Any action touching SVR-adjacent infrastructure carries escalation risk at the sovereign intelligence relationship level.',
    success:'SVR-adjacent actors and infrastructure correctly excluded from ransomware pressure campaigns. No disruption action inadvertently targets SVR-linked infrastructure.' },

  { id:'genpros', conf:'CONFIRMED', label:['Prosecutor','General'],  module:'RU', nodeRef:'GenPros', layer:'state', tier:'HIGH',   replace:'LOW', backfire:'LOW',    protected:false, owner:'Prosecution bottleneck', phase:null, jurisdiction:null,
    desc:'Institutional bottleneck that prevents criminal prosecution of ransomware actors even when MVD and FNS have built viable cases.',
    role:'The Russian Prosecutor General holds authority over cases that intersect with national security interests. When FSB frames a cybercrime case as touching state interests, Prosecutor General review effectively blocks conviction regardless of evidence quality. This explains why MVD arrests of ransomware actors rarely produce meaningful sentences. The 2021 REvil arrests are the most studied example: arrests occurred, prosecution stalled.',
    lever:'Structure MVD referrals to minimise national security framing: tax evasion and money laundering charges via the FNS and MVD pipeline are harder to block at this stage than cybercrime charges. Document blocked cases as confirmatory evidence of active FSB protection. Calibrate partner government expectations on Russian cooperation accordingly.',
    targets:'The institutional bottleneck mechanism rather than individuals. Cases that disappear at Prosecutor General review confirm active protection for the relevant actor. Build a pattern-of-practice record for multilateral pressure.',
    constraints:'No external lever operates against the Prosecutor General directly. Post-2022 political environment has made the bottleneck more reliable, not less. Western mutual legal assistance requests now face this bottleneck as a near-universal barrier for FSB-adjacent actors.',
    success:'Referrals structured to avoid automatic national security blocking. Blocked case patterns documented and used to confirm protection relationships. Partner governments calibrated on realistic prosecution expectations.' },

    { id:'fns',   conf:'ANALYST',   label:['FNS','Tax Exposure'], module:'RU',nodeRef:'FNS',    layer:'state', tier:'HIGH', replace:'LOW', backfire:'LOW',     protected:false, owner:'Domestic tax lever',    phase:null, jurisdiction:null, desc:'Lifestyle and undeclared income exposure channel that creates low-blowback pressure.', role:'Russia Federal Tax Service. No arrest power. Value is as an exposure and referral node feeding MVD without geopolitical sensitivity. Exploits the structural gap between ransomware earnings and declared income — enormous for most mid-to-high-tier actors living in Russia.', lever:'Lifestyle intelligence packages from open Russian registries: property records, vehicle registrations, corporate interests, travel patterns. FNS referrals to MVD for laundering charges do not require identifying the predicate cybercrime.', targets:'Mid-to-high-tier actors with domestic Russian lifestyle exposure. FSB officers with asset portfolios inconsistent with declared government salaries. Shell company operators via Art. 199 corporate tax evasion.', constraints:'FNS alone will not produce incapacitation. Intelligence products must carry no Western sourcing fingerprint. Political greenlight required for regime-adjacent actors.', success:'Actors become domestic financial liabilities. MVD file opened via laundering referral. FSB officer protecting the actor faces lifestyle exposure that competing factions can use.' },

  { id:'doxing', conf:'ANALYST', label:['Exposure /','Doxxing Actors'], module:'EXO', nodeRef:'Exo 01', layer:'exo', tier:'HIGH', replace:'LOW', backfire:'MEDIUM', protected:false, owner:'Non-state / independent', phase:null, jurisdiction:null,
    desc:'Non-state exposure and doxxing actors: leakers, insiders, and independent researchers who destroy criminal trust infrastructure from outside both the ecosystem and law enforcement. Modeled as exogenous pressure on the ecosystem, not part of it.',
    role:'A pressure class that no agency runs and no framework controls, with a documented kill record. The Black Basta chat leak (February 2025, ExploitWhispers, 196,045 internal messages) killed the brand with zero law-enforcement action; the group is defunct 18 months with no successor brand (Confirmed). The Conti leaks (2022) set the precedent: reconstitution failed because mutual trust was destroyed, not capacity. GangExposed published identity dossiers on Conti and TrickBot leadership in May 2025 using OSINT, darknet database purchases, and a claimed leaked FSB border-control database; the Kovalev identification was independently corroborated by BKA (Confirmed for Kovalev, Credible for the rest). The Gentlemen backend breach and public identification of the operator (May-June 2026) opened a documented 60-90 day pressure window (Credible). Framing these actors as a distinct lane parallel to the state lane is Analyst inference.',
    lever:'Information operations against internal trust (leaks, doxxing, honeypot doubt) are a high-ROI complement to takedowns: trust destruction kills brands faster than infrastructure seizure (Confirmed across ALPHV, Black Basta, and XSS). The actionable lever is exploitation of exposure windows while they are fresh: indictment plus Red Notice inside the window before OPSEC rebuild or rebrand completes.',
    targets:'Trust-dependent nodes: RaaS brands, underground forums, and leak-site operations. Brands die of trust destruction faster than of infrastructure loss; forums lose engagement on honeypot suspicion alone.',
    constraints:'These actors are independent and unverifiable: methods, motives, funding, and reliability sit outside any framework control, and identifications require independent corroboration before action is sequenced on them. Exposure can also tip actors into hardening, rebrand, or state protection before enforcement is ready. This lane cannot be tasked, only anticipated and exploited.',
    success:'Brand collapse without enforcement expenditure (Black Basta model), and exposure windows converted into custody, designation, or indictment before they close (the open test case is The Gentlemen window).',
    monthlyEvents:[{month:'2026-05',status:'NOTABLE',label:'The Gentlemen backend breach exposes admin identity and affiliate TOX IDs',detail:'The May 4 backend breach of The Gentlemen (leaked Rocket database, 16.22 GB) exposed the admin identity zeta88/hastalamuerte, a confirmed TOX ID, and 8 affiliate TOX IDs across 29 campaigns. A non-LE exposure event opening a 30-60 day enforcement window before operational security rebuild: the same class of trust destruction that killed Black Basta and Conti, this time before brand death rather than after.',conf:'CONFIRMED'},{month:'2026-06',status:'NOTABLE',label:'The Gentlemen operator publicly identified by independent reporting; pressure window open',detail:'Brian Krebs publicly identified the operator of the number-two YTD RaaS as Alexander Andreevich Yapaev of Izhevsk, Russia, with affiliate TOX IDs exposed. Pressure window assessed at 60-90 days before OPSEC hardening or rebrand per the Embargo-to-ArmCorp-to-Gentlemen precedent. Independent-researcher exposure, not law enforcement action: the defining mechanism of this lane.',conf:'CREDIBLE'}] },

  { id:'enab-transit', conf:'CONFIRMED', label:['Upstream Transit','Carriers'], module:'ENAB', nodeRef:'Enab 01', layer:'enab', tier:'CRITICAL', replace:'LOW', backfire:'MEDIUM', protected:false, owner:'Carriers / voluntary action', phase:null, jurisdiction:'western',
    desc:'Legitimate transit carriers and network operators whose route announcements keep bulletproof hosting reachable. Without a transit provider announcing its routes, a bulletproof host is offline regardless of how many servers it owns. Modeled as western commercial enablement: permission, not protection.',
    why:'Permission and protection are two different mechanisms. The Russian state lane explains why BPH operators are not arrested; the western commercial layer explains why their servers stay online (Analyst inference grounded in Confirmed dependency mapping). Transit is the only BPH dependency whose removal is instant, comprehensive, and effective across every hosted service simultaneously (Confirmed). These are legitimate businesses inside western jurisdictions, which is precisely why they are viable leverage points.',
    role:'The single largest structural vulnerability above the BPH layer. Recorded Future Insikt Group identified more than a dozen assessed threat activity enablers taking upstream connectivity from one German carrier, aurologic GmbH, including sanctioned Aeza; roughly 50 percent of Aeza International announced prefixes still routed via aurologic after the US, UK, and Australian designations, and several downstream networks route exclusively through it (Confirmed). Unilateral action displaces rather than removes: UK provider DataCamp Limited terminated its Aeza contract after the July 2024 Qurium Doppelganger report, and Aeza publicly stated it moved to aurologic and continued operating (Confirmed). No public reporting alleges that aurologic, Hetzner, Hurricane Electric, Zayo, Belcloud, Hostkey, or MIRhosting knowingly facilitates crime; the assessment concerns structural position and demonstrated non-intervention, not intent (Confirmed caveat carried from the enablement analysis).',
    lever:'Coordinated upstream refusal by the realistic set of alternative carriers, not termination by one. The November 2008 McColo de-peering by Global Crossing and Hurricane Electric removed the network within a day and cut global spam volume by two thirds or more; no comparable voluntary upstream action against a major BPH has been documented since (Confirmed). Supporting levers: the Spamhaus facilitator model of SBL escalation against the carrier own network (Confirmed as published position, June 2026), and regulator-prompted carrier engagement, since under the DSA mere-conduit regime carriers have no general monitoring duty and act only on formal notice (Confirmed).',
    targets:'Jurisdiction decides where the lever exists. Available and unused against Aeza (aurologic, Hetzner, Hurricane Electric, Zayo) and DEDBROPRO (Belcloud); available against Stark/THE.Hosting (MIRhosting colocation reaching AMS-IX and DE-CIX Frankfurt) and ZServers (Hostkey); effectively absent against Media Land (three Russian peers plus one in Montenegro) and Bearhost/PROSPERO (Russian transit including a Kaspersky Lab ASN, a relationship Kaspersky denies is intentional) (Confirmed and Credible per the profile upstream tables).',
    constraints:'Entirely voluntary: no EU legal duty compels disconnection, and the DSA/DDG liability framework makes inaction the legally defensible position while proactive disconnection carries contractual and commercial risk (Confirmed). These are lawful companies not accused of wrongdoing: designating carriers would set precedent affecting legitimate European operators, and de-peering also cuts their lawful customers (constraint documented in the enablement analysis; the MEDIUM backfire rating is Analyst inference from it).',
    success:'Documented voluntary upstream terminations per year rising from the current near-zero; upstream diversity per profiled ASN shrinking under coordinated pressure rather than growing; a McColo-class coordinated refusal executed against a designated BPH with reachability loss measured in days.',
    hit:'Coordinated carrier de-peering on the McColo model, Spamhaus SBL facilitator escalation, regulator-prompted voluntary carrier action, and, at the political margin, designation of enablers with demonstrated repeat knowledge (Confirmed as documented leverage options; none currently exercised at scale).',
    damage:'The host goes unreachable across every hosted service simultaneously; alternatives are finite, and each forced relocation degrades peering quality and raises cost (McColo outcome Confirmed; the Aeza-to-aurologic displacement pattern Confirmed).',
    monthlyEvents:[{month:'2026-06',status:'NOTABLE',label:'Spamhaus formalizes the facilitator framing: carriers, IP brokers, datacenters (Jun 11)',detail:'The Spamhaus Project published its facilitator argument on June 11, 2026: network carriers, IP address brokers, and datacenters provide services that are essential, difficult to obtain independently, and available from a finite number of sources, and pursuing individual bulletproof hosts without pursuing facilitators treats symptoms rather than causes. Stated mechanism: SBL escalation against the facilitator own network, with law-enforcement attractiveness rising as bulletproof customers cluster. The first formal industry articulation of the transit-layer leverage this lane models.',conf:'CONFIRMED'},{month:'2026-07',status:'NOTABLE',label:'Media Land peers hold through sanctions, indictment, and EU designation',detail:'All four observed Media Land transit peers (NetCom-R, MegaFon, SYSECT D.O.O., Lavrentyev A.A.) remained in place through the July 13 EU designation and the July 14 unsealed N.D. Ohio indictment, as they had through the November 2025 OFAC action. Three of the four sit in Russia and one in Montenegro, so the western transit lever is effectively absent for this provider: the clearest documented illustration that transit pressure is a jurisdiction-dependent tool, not a universal one.',conf:'CONFIRMED'}] },

  { id:'enab-registry', conf:'CONFIRMED', label:['Registries &','Sponsoring LIRs'], module:'ENAB', nodeRef:'Enab 02', layer:'enab', tier:'HIGH', replace:'LOW', backfire:'LOW-MED', protected:false, owner:'RIPE NCC / registry community', phase:null, jurisdiction:'western',
    desc:'Regional internet registries and sponsoring local internet registries that keep bulletproof hosting addressed: IP prefixes and autonomous system numbers issued by RIPE NCC directly, or indirectly through sponsoring LIRs that assign sub-allocations.',
    why:'Address space is the dependency that determines whether a designated provider can reconstitute. Sanctions designate the entity, not the infrastructure: because connectivity attaches to ASNs and prefixes while sanctions attach to legal persons, and registry policy permits the designated party itself to separate the two, a designation removes a name from a contract without removing a route from the global table (Confirmed pattern across every documented case in the BPH library; the framing is carried from the enablement analysis).',
    role:'RIPE NCC, on guidance from the Dutch Ministry of Foreign Affairs, treats registration of number resources as an economic resource that must be frozen on EU designation, but does not deregister resources or terminate membership: the sanctioned provider keeps its ASNs and prefixes and existing space keeps routing (Confirmed). LIR autonomy is the reconstitution mechanism: register a new entity, assign the space to it, update the record, each step procedurally ordinary. Stark migrated AS44477 to freshly created RIPE organization PQ Hosting Plus S.R.L. on May 16, 2025, days before the EU designation; Aeza began reallocating US-facing resources to Serbian entity Smart Digital Ideas DOO within 24 hours of the July 2025 OFAC action, and RIR revocations for sanctions stand at zero (Confirmed). Sponsoring LIRs in permissive jurisdictions have repeatedly supplied space to networks that could not obtain it directly, including at least one registration built on a falsified end-user agreement that a basic public-records check would have caught (Confirmed).',
    lever:'Registration integrity enforcement under existing RIPE-858 powers: deregistration for falsified information, corporate impersonation, or non-payment is already permitted and terminal where pursued, requiring detection capacity rather than new rules (Confirmed). The larger levers are policy reform extending deregistration triggers to sanctions designation and tightened sponsoring-LIR identity verification, both documented as proposals rather than practice (Confirmed as proposals in the enablement analysis).',
    targets:'Sponsoring LIRs with prior abuse association (a Turkish LIR sponsoring several suspicious ASNs all created during 2025 is documented); ASN registrations built on impersonation of legitimate companies; the reallocation chains that carried Aeza space through Smart Digital Ideas DOO to Hypercore Ltd (Confirmed).',
    constraints:'Registry neutrality is a stated institutional position: RIPE NCC holds that internet number resources should be kept separate from political disputes and has said it will continue to investigate a blanket exemption of number resources from EU sanctions regulation (Confirmed). Deregistration on designation alone is not current policy, and forcing it through the membership against that stance carries governance blowback; integrity enforcement against false paperwork does not (stated position Confirmed; the LOW-MED backfire rating is Analyst inference).',
    success:'Time from designation to first prefix reallocation lengthening from the current hours; deregistrations executed for falsified registration data rising from the current baseline; sponsoring-LIR verification catching registrations that public company records already contradict.',
    hit:'RIPE-858 deregistration for falsified data or impersonation, sponsoring-LIR verification tightening, registry policy reform adding designation as a deregistration trigger, and Dutch court orders, the one existing external compulsion path (Confirmed).',
    damage:'Reconstitution stops being a paperwork exercise: without transferable allocations, a designated provider cannot ride its own address space into a new shell, and the documented hours-long designation-to-reallocation cycle breaks (Analyst inference from the Confirmed reconstitution record).',
    monthlyEvents:[{month:'2026-05',status:'NOTABLE',label:'Stark seizure leaves the registry layer intact: 200+ prefixes still routing',detail:'The May 18-27 Dutch FIOD action seized 800+ servers at the physical layer, but more than 200 prefixes associated with the Stark successor cluster remained active afterwards. Seizure without deregistration or de-peering removes hardware while address allocations and routing relationships persist, consistent with RIPE NCC policy that freezes a sanctioned member resources but does not reclaim them. Physical action degrades capacity; the registry layer preserves reachability.',conf:'CONFIRMED'}] },

  { id:'enab-corp', conf:'CONFIRMED', label:['Corporate','Formation Agents'], module:'ENAB', nodeRef:'Enab 03', layer:'enab', tier:'MEDIUM', replace:'HIGH', backfire:'LOW', protected:false, owner:'Companies House / registrars', phase:null, jurisdiction:'western',
    desc:'Formation agents and company registries that keep bulletproof hosting incorporated. Registry membership, transit contracts, and datacenter agreements all require a legal entity; formation agents supply one within hours, typically at a virtual office address.',
    why:'This is the layer that converts a sanctions designation from a terminal event into a paperwork exercise (Confirmed characterization from the enablement analysis). It is also the one enabling layer where an existing, unglamorous western power has actually removed entities: company-register integrity enforcement has outperformed sanctions authority against the shells.',
    role:'The BPH corporate record is a formation-agent record. Stark Industries Solutions Ltd was incorporated at UK Companies House (13906017) on February 10, 2022, two weeks before the full-scale invasion of Ukraine; the cluster then layered PQ Hosting Plus S.R.L. (Moldova) and WorkTitans B.V. (Netherlands), inserting a new legal entity between operator and infrastructure at each rebrand (Confirmed). Aeza rotated through Smart Digital Ideas DOO (Serbia, registered hours after the July 2025 OFAC announcement), Hypercore Ltd (UK, registered July 3-7, 2025), and Aeza International Ltd (Confirmed). UK entities recur most frequently across the profiles, often at a small set of shared London virtual office addresses, and in at least two documented 2025 cases the name of a real, unrelated European business was used to register an ASN (Confirmed). Media Land marks the jurisdictional boundary: its corporate layer (Media Land LLC, St. Petersburg, registered October 2015, 100 percent owned by Volosovik) is Russian, so this western lever does not reach it (Confirmed).',
    lever:'Registration integrity enforcement, which has outperformed sanctions authority against the shells: Hypercore Ltd was struck off in December 2025 and Aeza International Ltd on March 24, 2026, both for false or misleading incorporation information under the Companies Act rather than under sanctions powers (Confirmed). UK Companies House identity verification for directors and persons of significant control began rolling out November 18, 2025; extending verification to formation agents and beneficial owners is the documented compounding step (rollout Confirmed; effect not yet measurable).',
    targets:'Formation agents and virtual office addresses reused across multiple assessed threat activity enablers; shell entities holding registry memberships and transit contracts for designated providers; incorporation filings containing false statements, the one trigger with a demonstrated terminal outcome.',
    constraints:'Highest replaceability in the lane: a struck-off shell is replaced in hours across UK, Netherlands, Serbia, Estonia, Seychelles, or US state registries, so entity removal is friction, not denial (Confirmed pattern). Verification burdens fall mostly on the legitimate majority of filings, but the enforcement record to date targeted demonstrably false filings, which keeps blowback low (strike-off record Confirmed; the LOW backfire rating is Analyst inference).',
    success:'Formation friction visible in the reconstitution clock: designation-to-new-entity time rising from the current hours-to-days; repeat strike-offs of successor shells; identity verification catching false incorporation before registry membership and transit contracts attach to the new entity.',
    hit:'Strike-off for false or misleading incorporation information, formation-agent and beneficial-owner identity verification, cross-registry sharing of the reused-address and impersonation patterns already documented in open reporting (Confirmed instruments; scaling Analyst inference).',
    damage:'Each rebrand cycle gets slower and costlier: without a fresh entity on demand, frozen resources cannot be re-homed, registry records cannot be updated, and the designation-survival playbook documented for Stark and Aeza loses its first step (Analyst inference from the Confirmed rebrand record).',
    monthlyEvents:[{month:'2026-03',status:'NOTABLE',label:'Aeza International Ltd struck off at Companies House (Mar 24)',detail:'Aeza International Ltd (company 15109642) was dissolved at UK Companies House on March 24, 2026, roughly six months after its UK designation and nine months after the OFAC action, for false or misleading incorporation information rather than under sanctions authority, following the December 23, 2025 strike-off of Hypercore Ltd under section 1002A of the Companies Act 2006 on the same ground. Registration integrity enforcement, not sanctions enforcement, is what has actually removed the shell entities in this layer.',conf:'CONFIRMED'}] }
];

